        "---\n",
        "title: \"Energy Conservation\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"ENCH291 — Lecture 1\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Laws of Thermodynamics\n",
        "The 1st Law of Thermodynamics = energy is conserved.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "- Energy is not created or destroyed\n",
        "- Lots of types of energy (potential, kinetic, internal)\n",
        "- Energy can be converted between different forms\n",
        "- Doesn't apply to nuclear processes\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible unless-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "This law states that energy is conserved but can be converted between many\n",
        "different forms. During these conversions, energy is neither created nor\n",
        "destroyed. In addition to this first law of thermodynamics, the second law\n",
        "imposes a further restriction: during any energy conversion process, a portion\n",
        "of the energy is dissipated into a less useful form, typically heat.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n",
        "### What about nuclear?\n",
        "OK — nuclear processes violate our simple 1st Law requirement that energy is\n",
        "conserved. Nuclear processes also violate the principle of conservation of mass.\n",
        "During nuclear reactions, mass is converted to energy. So what really matters\n",
        "is not conservation of mass or conservation of energy separately, but the\n",
        "conservation of total mass–energy. This is captured by Einstein's relation\n",
        "$E = mc^2$, which shows that mass is a form of energy.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Types of Energy\n",
        "\n",
        "There are many different types of energy relevant to chemical engineering. \n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "::: {.incremental}\n",
        "- Potential energy, $E_p = mgz$ \n",
        "- Kinetic energy, $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}m\\mathbf{v}^2$\n",
        "- Internal energy, $U$ = energy stored in matter\n",
        ":::\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible unless-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "The most common types of energy we deal with are potential energy, kinetic energy, and internal energy.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Potential Energy ($E_p$)\n",
        "Potential energy is the energy associated with the position of an object in a gravitational field. It is given by:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$E_p = mgz$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $m$ is mass, $g$ is gravitational acceleration, $z$ is height above a reference point.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Kinetic Energy ($E_k$)\n",
        "Kinetic energy is the energy associated with the motion of an object. It is given by:\n",
        "$$E_k = \\frac{1}{2}m\\mathbf{v}^2$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $m$ is mass and $\\mathbf{v}$ is velocity. Note that in this course we use the bold typeface $\\mathbf{v}$ to denote velocity. This is becuase we will use $v$ to denote specific volume (units of m³/kg or m³/mol) later on, and we want to avoid confusion.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Internal Energy ($U$)\n",
        "Internal energy is the energy stored within matter, including chemical bonds, molecular vibrations, and other microscopic forms of energy. \n",
        "\n",
        "While internal energy is the fundamental property by which energy is stored within matter, in chemical engineering we rarely deal with $U$ directly, but rather use enthalpy ($H$) as it is more convenient. We will discuss this in more detail later on. \n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        ". . .\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "### Key Term\n",
        "**Internal Energy** ($U$) — a thermodynamic state function; how energy is stored within matter.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- repeat slide title -->\n",
        "In many cases, potential and kinetic energy changes are negligible compared to changes in internal energy.\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- slide only bullet points -->\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "Exemptions may include:\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.incremental}\n",
        "- Pumping fluids. $E_p$ or $E_k$ terms may be significant\n",
        "- High-speed flows. e.g. gas turbines, where $E_k$ is significant\n",
        "- Large elevation changes, e.g. hydroelectric dams, where $E_p$ is significant\n",
        ":::\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- Book only content -->\n",
        "::: {.content-visible unless-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "This means we will often neglect $E_p$ and $E_k$ terms in our energy balances. This is a common assumption in chemical engineering applications, where fluid velocities are moderate and elevation differences are small. There are exceptions to this, such as in pumping fluids, high-speed flows (e.g. gas turbines), or large elevation changes (e.g. hydroelectric dams), where kinetic and potential energy changes can be significant and must be included in the energy balance.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
