        "## Energy Changes vs Absolute Energies\n",
        "\n",
        "In chemical engineering, energy changes are the critical values, not absolute energies. \n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- Slide only bullet points -->\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "::: {.incremental}\n",
        "- Energy changes determine process design and operation\n",
        "- We never measure absolute energies\n",
        "- Any \"absolute\" internal energy value is actually a relative value with an arbitrary reference point\n",
        ":::\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- Book only content -->\n",
        "::: {.content-visible unless-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "The reason for this is that we are often interested in how much energy is required to carry out a process, or how much energy is released by a reaction, rather than the absolute energy content of a system. For example, when designing a chemical reactor, we need to know how much energy is released by the reaction to ensure that we can safely remove it and maintain the desired temperature. Similarly, when designing a heat exchanger, we need to know how much energy needs to be transferred to achieve the desired temperature change. In both cases, it is the change in energy that matters, not the absolute value.\n",
        "\n",
        "In addition it is important to realise, we can only measure energy changes, not absolute energies. For example, we can measure the heat released by a reaction, but we cannot directly measure the internal energy of a system. This is why we focus on energy changes in chemical engineering.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## State functions, properties and changes\n",
        "\n",
        "State properties are are critical aspect of energy balances. \n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- Slide only bullet points -->\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "State proerties are:\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.incremental}\n",
        "- Thermodynamic properties that define the state of a system\n",
        "- Functions of state variables (e.g. temperature, pressure, composition)\n",
        "- Examples include temperature, pressure, internal energy, enthalpy, Gibbs energy\n",
        "- State changes are changes in these properties during a process\n",
        ":::\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- Book only content -->\n",
        "::: {.content-visible unless-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "Without certain properites being state functions, we would not be able to determine how much energy is stored in a system, or how much energy is required to change the state of a system. State properties are fundamental to our ability to perform energy balances and design chemical processes.\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the internal energy $U$ of a system. While we might not be able to measure the absolute value of $U$, as it is a state function, if have a system at a certain temperature, pressure and composition, if we were to change the temperature, pressure or composition to some other value, then return the system to its original state, the internal energy would return to its original value. This is a key aspect of state functions. So while we cannot measure the absolute value of $U$, we normally can measure changes in $U$ when we change the state of the system. So we don't know $U_1$ or $U_2$, but we can measure $\\Delta U = U_2 - U_1$.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- repeat slide title -->\n",
        "\n",
        "Imagine we change the temperature of a system and measure the $\\Delta U$\n",
        "\n",
        "![$U$ is a state function so we can measure or calculate the difference between two states](figs/U_statefunction_diagram.png){width=70% fig-align=\"center\" #fig-U_statefunction_diagram}\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- Book only content -->\n",
        "::: {.content-visible unless-format=\"revealjs\"}\n",
        "In @fig-U_statefunction_diagram, we have a system that starts at state 1 with internal energy $U_1$. We then change the temperature of the system to some other value, which changes the internal energy to $U_2$. Because $U$ is a state function, if we were to return the system to its original temperature, the internal energy would return to $U_1$. This means that we can measure or calculate the change in internal energy $\\Delta U = U_2 - U_1$, even though we cannot measure the absolute values of $U_1$ or $U_2$.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n"
